    "final_answer": "I have gathered comprehensive information about the T5 pre-trained language model. Let me provide my findings.\n\n## Final Answer\n\n**Example of a Pre-trained Language Model in NLP: `google-t5/t5-small` (T5 Small)**\n\n### Model Overview\n- **Full Name**: Text-To-Text Transfer Transformer (T5)\n- **Developed by**: Google (Colin Raffel, Noam Shazeer, Adam Roberts, Katherine Lee, Sharan Narang, Michael Matena, Yanqi Zhou, Wei Li, Peter J. Liu)\n- **Model Size**: 60 million parameters (60.5M)\n- **Model Type**: Language model\n- **Languages Supported**: English, French, Romanian, German (5 languages total)\n- **License**: Apache 2.0\n- **Monthly Downloads**: ~24 million\n- **Training Dataset**: c4 (Colossal Clean Crawled Corpus)\n\n### Tasks Specifically Designed For\n\nAccording to the model card on Hugging Face:\n\n> *\"With T5, we propose reframing all NLP tasks into a unified text-to-text-format where the input and output are always text strings... Our text-to-text framework allows us to use the same model, loss function, and hyperparameters on any NLP task.\"*\n\nThe model is specifically tagged/designed for the following tasks:\n\n| Task | Type |\n|------|------|\n| **Translation** | Primary task (visible as the main tag on the model page) |\n| **Summarization** | Pipeline tag |\n| **Text-to-Text Generation** | Pipeline tag (`text2text-generation`) |\n| **Text Generation Inference** | Pipeline tag |\n\n### Key Distinction\nUnlike BERT-style models which can only output a class label or a span of input text, T5 unifies all NLP tasks into a text-to-text format, making it suitable for a wide range of NLP applications including translation, text summarization, question answering, and other text generation tasks using a single unified model architecture.\n\n**Source**: Retrieved from https://huggingface.co/google-t5/t5-small",
